# Nginx `location =` vs `location /`

In nginx, `location =` and `location /` control how precisely a URL must match and which block wins when multiple locations could apply.

---

## `location = /something` — Exact Match

- Matches only the exact URI
- Has the highest priority
- Stops searching once matched

Example:

    location = /login {
        return 403;
    }

Matches:
    /login

Does NOT match:
    /login/
    /login?x=1
    /login/extra

### When to use `location =`
- You need to target one specific path
- You want it to override all other locations
- Common use cases:
  - Redirects
  - Health checks
  - Security rules
  - Performance optimizations for hot paths

Example:

    location = / {
        return 301 /app;
    }

---

## `location /` — Prefix (Catch-All) Match

- Matches anything starting with `/`
- Lowest specificity
- Acts as the default handler

Example:

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;
    }

Matches:
    /
    /about
    /images/logo.png
    /api/v1/users

### When to use `location /`
- As a fallback
- To serve a website or SPA
- When you want to handle “everything else”

---

## Location Matching Priority (Important)

nginx evaluates locations in this order:

1. Exact match — `location =`
2. Longest prefix match — `location /foo/bar`
3. Regex match — `location ~` or `location ~*`
4. Fallback prefix — `location /`

Example configuration:

    location = /health { return 200; }
    location /api/ { proxy_pass http://api; }
    location / { root /var/www; }

Request → Matched Location:
    /health      → location = /health
    /api/users  → location /api/
    /about      → location /

---

## Rule of Thumb

- Use `location =` for special, exact paths
- Use `location /` as your default catch-all


